  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 215 AM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nWarming trend continues today and tomorrow due to some height rises \nover the region. By Friday, 850mb temperatures reach the 5-10 \nCelsius range, around the 90th percentile for this time of year. As \na result, highs climb into the 40s today and the upper 40s to mid \n50s Friday (about 10-15 degrees above late-November normals).\nSaturday, a low pressure center passing to the north will drag a \ncold front across the forecast area. Behind the front temperatures \nwill cool off for a bit for the weekend and into the start of next \nweek, with highs in the upper 30s to low 40s expected through at \nleast the start of next week (near-normal to slightly above normal). \n\nDue to the influence of the aforementioned low, rain chances will \ndevelop along a boundary on Friday. Rain will move from northwest to \nsoutheast across the forecast area, beginning Friday morning and \nexiting the forecast area late Friday afternoon. Ice forms aloft, \nbut the lowest ~2km will be above freezing, allowing for plenty of \ntime to melt before falling to the surface as rain. There is a brief \nperiod over northeastern South Dakota where freezing rain will be \npossible, but the potential will be highly dependent on both the \nspeed of the cold front as well as how cold the near-surface \ntemperatures get. Overall expecting little in the way of \naccumulations due to a near-surface dry layer (that same lowest 2km \nhousing the warm layer). Given that, rain may struggle to reach the \nsurface at first, but with enough residence time rain may still be \nable to saturate the profile. \n\nBehind the cold front, winds turn westerly and increase. The \nstrongest gusts Friday afternoon are anticipated west of the \nMissouri River. NBM probabilities of reaching Wind Advisory criteria \n(45 miles per hour) continue to decrease, now with some isolated \npockets of 10-20% chances. Strong winds will continue Saturday \nafternoon, with gusts of 30-40 miles per hour once again expected \nalong and west of the Missouri River. Wind Advisory probabilities \nare a bit stronger Saturday afternoon, with a widespread 10-20% \nchance west of the Missouri River and pockets up to 30-45%. Will \ncontinue to keep an eye on how this Wind Advisory potential evolves \nas these conditions approach. Both afternoons, the strong winds will \npush Grassland Fire Danger in the High category over much of central \nand north central South Dakota.",
